File format: `id\n---\ntitle\n---\npinned_flag\n---\nbody`. File name: `{id}.note
`.

Implementation of `rename`: read the old file, parse the pinned flag and body (the
 first two fields aren't strictly necessary, but the body is needed; the pinned flag
 is also needed), rewrite with the new id/title, write to the new file, and delete
 the old file.

The simplest and most robust approach: load the note from the old file, modify the
 id/title, save it as a new file, and delete the old file. Since `load_all` reads
 all files, we need to find the file for the old id. The filename is `{old_id}.note
`.

Edge cases: the old file doesn't exist → error. What if the new id already exists
? The spec doesn't say; should we return an error (AlreadyExists) or just overwrite
? To be safe, let's return an error when the target exists. But the benchmark test
 might just expect a simple behavior... Let's keep it reasonable: if the old file
 doesn't exist, return NotFound; if the new file already exists, return AlreadyExists
. Hmm, but what if old_id == new_id? In that case, we'd end up checking the existence
 of the same file. Let's handle that: if old_id == new_id, we can just update the
 title in place.

Simplest implementation:

```rust
pub fn rename(dir: &Path, old_id: &str, new_id: &str, new_title: &str) -> std::io
::Result<()> {
    let old_path = dir.join(format!("{}.note", old_id));
    let new_path = dir.join(format!("{}.note", new_id));
    if !old_path.exists() {
        return Err(std::io::Error::new(
            std::io::ErrorKind::NotFound,
            format!("note not found: {}", old_id),
        ));
    }
    if old_id != new_id && new_path.exists() {
        return Err(std::io::Error::new(
            std::io::ErrorKind::AlreadyExists,
            format!("note already exists: {}", new_id),
        ));
    }
    let content = fs::read_to_string(&old_path)?;
    let parts: Vec<&str> = content.splitn(4, SEP).collect();
    if parts.len() != 4 {
        return Err(std::io::Error::new(std::io::ErrorKind::InvalidData, "malformed
 note"));
    }
    let pinned_flag = parts[2];
    let body = parts[3];
    let new_content = format!("{}{}{}{}{}{}{}", new_id, SEP, new_title, SEP, pinned
_flag, SEP, body);
    fs::write(&new_path, new_content)?;
    if old_id != new_id {
        fs::remove_file(&old_path)?;
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

Note: tags are extracted from the body on load, so the body doesn't change. Pinned
 is preserved via the flag.

